	% Slide 2: Introduction - Borderline Predatory EB1A Journals and the Grand Strategy of Profile Building
	\begin{frame}{Borderline Predatory EB1A Journals\\and the Grand Strategy of Profile Building}
		\begin{block}{}
			I want to discuss some journals that were previously indexed and are commonly used by many people trying to build their profiles for EB1A applications. There is nothing illegal about publishing in these journals, and I am not saying they are completely worthless. However, in my opinion, they do not move the needle enough for most EB1A applicants when viewed as part of a broader strategy.
		\end{block}
		\vspace{0.3cm}
		I will try to put this discussion into the context of the larger EB1A game plan.
	\end{frame}
	
	% Slide 3: The 40/60 Rule
	\begin{frame}{The EB1A Equation}
		\begin{columns}
			\column{0.5\textwidth}
			\begin{block}{Profile Building}
				\Huge 30-40\%
			\end{block}
			In my view, profile building accounts for roughly 30–40\% of an EB1A case.
			\column{0.5\textwidth}
			\begin{block}{Legal Strategy}
				\Huge 60-70\%
			\end{block}
			The remaining 60–70\% comes from legal strategy, which I discuss in my Long Game course, including attorney selection, motions, pre-litigation, litigation, settlements, and related topics.
		\end{columns}
		\vspace{0.5cm}
		For a moment, let's focus on profile building, which I estimate to be about 40\% of the equation.
	\end{frame}
	
	% Slide 4: The Cost-Benefit Reality
	\begin{frame}{The Cost-Benefit Reality}
		\begin{alertblock}{The Numbers}
			Assume an applicant needs a profile strength of 100 points before filing. Publishing 10–15 articles in the journals listed below may improve the profile by only about 5–7 points, while costing approximately \$5,000 in APCs and related expenses.
		\end{alertblock}
		\vspace{0.3cm}
		\begin{block}{Additional Investment}
			This is 5k spend and 15 paper are just the starting, now more effort have to be invested in manual indexing and other indexing activities so that it create stronger evidence in other categories, the overall return may be much higher.
		\end{block}
	\end{frame}
	
	% Slide 5: Borderline Journals
	\begin{frame}{Borderline Journals}
		\begin{block}{Financial Considerations}
			If you do not have substantial financial resources, I generally recommend avoiding expensive journals that provide only limited value for an EB1A case. But if you make over 150k, being cheap is detrimental.
		\end{block}
		\vspace{0.3cm}
		\begin{itemize}
			\item These journals may move the needle slightly, but not by much.
			\item If you have significant financial resources, there is nothing wrong with publishing in them.
			\item However, for individuals on a limited budget who are pursuing EB1A, the cost-benefit ratio is often unfavorable.
			\item If you have the time, you may be better off targeting stronger journals and avoiding publication venues that have become heavily saturated with EB1A applicants.
		\end{itemize}
		\vspace{0.3cm}
		Publishing the paper is only the beginning. The more important question is what impact that publication generates afterward.
	\end{frame}
	
	% Slide 6: Looking Beyond Just Publications
	\begin{frame}{Looking Beyond Just Publications}
		\begin{block}{}
			I discuss government indexing, review profiles, citation strategies, and other factors in my broader discussions on indexing. A paper should not be evaluated merely by whether it gets published. Instead, applicants should consider how it contributes to the overall profile through proper indexing support.
		\end{block}
		\vspace{0.3cm}
		For example:
		\begin{itemize}
			\item Government indexing and citations from government agencies, universities, and other respected institutions (25\%)
			\item Reviewing opportunities, including grant review activities (10\%)
			\item Mid-level awards and recognitions (10\%)
			\item Media mentions and news coverage (10\%)
			\item Letters from Members of Congress or other established opinion leaders (15\%)
			\item Critical or leading roles within organizations (10\%)
			\item Other strategic factors (10\%)
		\end{itemize}
	\end{frame}
	
	% Slide 7: Strategic Publication Value
	\begin{frame}{Strategic Publication Value}
		\begin{block}{}
			A publication should ideally create opportunities across several of these categories rather than simply increasing the publication count.
		\end{block}
		\vspace{0.3cm}
		\begin{exampleblock}{If Your Paper is NOT Leading To:}
			\begin{itemize}
				\item Reviewing invitations
				\item Citations from government agencies
				\item Inclusion in government indexes
				\item Recognition from EOLs
			\end{itemize}
		\end{exampleblock}
		\vspace{0.3cm}
		\centering
		\textcolor{myred}{\Large Then you are probably not using the paper strategically.}
		\vspace{0.3cm}
		\begin{block}{}
			How that has to be learned.
		\end{block}
	\end{frame}
	
	% Slide 8: The Legal Component
	\begin{frame}{The Legal Component}
		\begin{block}{}
			It is also important to remember that profile building is only part of the process.
		\end{block}
		\vspace{0.3cm}
		In my view, approximately 60\% of the outcome depends on legal strategy, including:
		\begin{itemize}
			\item Attorney selection
			\item Case presentation
			\item Evidence organization
			\item Narrative development
			\item Strategic use of accomplishments across multiple criteria
		\end{itemize}
		\vspace{0.3cm}
		\centering
		\textit{“The same profile can produce very different outcomes depending on how effectively it is presented.”}
	\end{frame}
	
	% Slide 9: Final Thoughts
	\begin{frame}{Final Thoughts}
		\begin{block}{}
			The journals listed below are not necessarily the worst journals, nor are they automatically useless. However, many have become heavily utilized by EB1A applicants. Some were indexed in the past and continue to attract submissions because of that history.
		\end{block}
		\vspace{0.3cm}
		They may provide some value and may move the needle slightly, but generally not enough to serve as a primary EB1A strategy on their own.
		\vspace{0.3cm}
		\begin{exampleblock}{The Real Goal}
			The goal should not be to accumulate publications. The goal should be to build a profile that generates recognition, influence, citations, review opportunities, media attention, and evidence supporting multiple EB1A criteria simultaneously.
		\end{exampleblock}
	\end{frame}
	
